### Step 1: Identify forces and conditions for circular motion

For a particle of mass 'm' and charge '\(-q\)' to move in a circle of radius 'r' around an infinitely long line charge of linear charge density '\(+\lambda\)', the electrostatic force provides the necessary centripetal force.
F_e = \frac{mv^2}{r}
The electric field 'E' due to an infinitely long line charge at a distance 'r' is given by:
E = \frac{2k\lambda}{r}
The electrostatic force on the charge '\(-q\)' is \(F_e = qE\). Equating this to the centripetal force:
qE = \frac{mv^2}{r}

### Step 2: Substitute E and solve for velocity (v)

Substitute the expression for E into the force equation:
q \left(\frac{2k\lambda}{r}\right) = \frac{mv^2}{r}
Simplify and solve for 'v':
v^2 = \frac{2kq\lambda}{m}
v = \sqrt{\frac{2kq\lambda}{m}}

### Step 3: Calculate the time period (T)

The time period 'T' for circular motion is given by the circumference divided by the velocity:
T = \frac{2\pi r}{v}
Substitute the expression for 'v':
T = \frac{2\pi r}{\sqrt{\frac{2kq\lambda}{m}}}
Rearrange the expression:
T = 2\pi r \sqrt{\frac{m}{2kq\lambda}}
The final expression for the time period matches option (2).

### Step 1: Analyze the forces on the block

From the free body diagram, the normal force \(N\) acting on the block is balanced by the component of gravity perpendicular to the incline. Given mass \(m = 1 \text{ kg}\) and \(g = 10 \text{ m/s}^2\), the gravitational force is \(mg = 1 \times 10 = 10 \text{ N}\).
N = mg \cos60^\circ
N = 10 \times \frac{1}{2} = 5 \text{ N}

### Step 2: Calculate the kinetic frictional force

The kinetic frictional force \(f_k\) is given by \(\mu_k N\). The problem states \(\mu_s = 0.1\), which implies \(\mu_k\) is also \(0.1\) (or less, but usually taken as the given coefficient if not specified otherwise for kinetic friction).
f_k = \mu_k N
f_k = 0.1 \times 5 = 0.5 \text{ N}

### Step 3: Calculate the work done against frictional force

The block is pushed up by a distance \(S = 10 \text{ m}\) along the inclined surface. The work done by friction is \(W_{friction} = -f_k \cdot S\) because friction opposes the motion. The work done *against* frictional force is the negative of the work done *by* friction.
W_{\text{against friction}} = -W_{friction}
W_{\text{against friction}} = -(-f_k \cdot S)
W_{\text{against friction}} = f_k \cdot S
W_{\text{against friction}} = 0.5 \text{ N} \times 10 \text{ m}
W_{\text{against friction}} = 5 \text{ J}
The work done against frictional force is 5 J.

